.SH NAME
tcaboot \- Trusted Collective (TC) authority initialization utility
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IX Header "SYNOPSIS"
\&\fBtcaboot\fR \fImulticast_group_number_for_TC_bulletins\fR \fImulticast_group_number_for_TC_records\fR \fInumber_of_authorities_in_collective\fR \fIK\fR \fIR\fR [ \fIdelay\fR ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.IX Header "DESCRIPTION"
\&\fBtcaboot\fR writes a TC authority administration command file that initializes
a TC authority database.  The file, named "boot.tcarc", is written to the
current working directory.  It simply contains two authority configuration
commands that initialize the TC authority database for the TC application
and then set the initial bulletin compilation time for this authority to
the current ctime plus \fIdelay\fR seconds.  If omitted, \fIdelay\fR defaults
to 5.  The other command-line arguments for \fBtcaboot\fR are discussed in
the descriptions of application initialization commands for \fBtcaadmin\fR;
see the \fBtcarc\fR\|(5) manual page for details.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.IX Header "EXIT STATUS"
.IP """0""" 4
.IX Item """0"""
Successful generation of TC authority initialization file.
.SH EXAMPLES
.IX Header "EXAMPLES"
.IP "tcaboot 210 209 6 50 .2" 4
.IX Item "tcaboot 210 209 6 50 .2"
Writes a boot.tcarc file that initializes the local node's TC authority
database as indicated and sets the next bulletin compilation time to the
current time plus 5 seconds.
.IP "tcaboot 210 209 6 50 .2 90" 4
.IX Item "tcaboot 210 209 6 50 .2 90"
Writes a boot.tcarc file that initializes the local node's TC authority
database as indicated and sets the next bulletin compilation time to the
current time plus 90 seconds.
.SH FILES
.IX Header "FILES"
No files apply.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.IX Header "ENVIRONMENT"
No environment variables apply.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.IX Header "DIAGNOSTICS"
The following diagnostics may be issued to the log file:
.IP "Can't open cmd file" 4
.IX Item "Can't open cmd file"
\&\fBtcaboot\fR is unable to create a file named boot.tcarc for the indicated reason,
a system error.
.IP "Can't write to cmd file" 4
.IX Item "Can't write to cmd file"
\&\fBtcaboot\fR is unable to write to boot.tcarc for the indicated reason,
a system error.
